 "text": "Lludd names two kindred figures. One is a Welsh saint or goddess, St. Lludd, whom Christianity made a human holy one. The source holds that her head was cut for her sanctity and rolled down a hill, and where it stopped a healing spring broke out. The other is a Welsh god or hero, Lludd Llaw Ereint, also told as NUDD. He was an ancient British king whose brother LLEFELYS held the Continent. The pair were sons of BELI, the god of death. Geoffrey of Monmouth says Lludd built London, and a temple to the old god stood by Ludgate where St Paul's now stands. In his time three plagues struck Britain: the Coriannead, a breed of demons, two DRAGONS, and a GIANT magician. Lludd and his brother ended all three. William Shakespeare is held to have drawn on such a king for Lear, but he mistook the name and called him LLYR.",
